  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=2$ intersects the line $y=2x+1.$",
  "worked_solution": "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=2x+1$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(2x+1-2)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(2x-1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+9+4{x}^{2}-4x+1 & =2 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-10x+8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-10)^{2}-4\\cdot 5\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =100-160 \\\\  & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-60<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $5{x}^{2}-10x+8=0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the circle $(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=2$ does not intersect the line $y=2x+1.$ \n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/56e313584d7de2f1c40302135b00ef80a1d3c1e920407c9c4a0c62c3c6769456.png)",
